    summary: "Macropa (N,N'-bis[(6-carboxy-2-pyridyl)methyl]-4,13-diaza-18-crown-6) is an 18-membered diaza-crown-6 macrocycle with two pendant picolinate arms, first reported by Thiele et al. in 2017. Unlike DOTA, whose rigid octadentate cavity favours small lanthanide-sized ions, macropa shows 'reverse size selectivity': it binds large ions (Ac3+, Pb2+, La3+, Ra2+) markedly more tightly than small ones, matching Ac3+'s large 1.12 Å ionic radius. In side-by-side testing, macropa complexed essentially all of a 26 kBq 225Ac spike within 5 minutes at room temperature at submicromolar ligand concentration, whereas DOTA complexed only around 10% under the same conditions and required 80°C to go to completion.\n\nBifunctional derivatives (macropa-NCS) have been conjugated to antibodies (trastuzumab, the GPC3-targeting antibody codrituzumab/GC33) and to the PSMA-targeting small molecule RPS-070, and the resulting 225Ac complexes were stable for 7-8 days against human serum and excess La3+ challenge. Rigidified analogues (H2BZmacropa, H2BZ2macropa) trade some thermodynamic stability for improved synthetic access. Macropa also complexes 212Pb, 133La, 213Bi, 223Ra and 224Ra with varying efficiency, making it one of two chelators (alongside DOTA) most discussed for 225Ac-based targeted alpha therapy as of 2026; it does not solve the separate daughter-recoil problem that follows 225Ac decay.",
