    summary: "Sarcophagine ('sar', 3,6,10,13,16,19-hexaazabicyclo[6.6.6]icosane) is a hexaazamacrobicyclic cage-amine ligand, developed originally by Sargeson and colleagues, whose fully encapsulating three-dimensional cage confers exceptional kinetic inertness on its copper(II) complexes. Because the copper ion is enclosed rather than merely chelated at a macrocycle rim, sarcophagine complexes form rapidly (typically 5-20 minutes) at room temperature across a broad pH range (4-9) and resist demetalation and reduction to Cu+ far more effectively than open-chain or single-ring chelators such as DTPA or NOTA. The bifunctional derivative MeCOSar (methyl-carboxylate sarcophagine) is the most widely used variant for peptide and antibody conjugation.\n\nSarcophagine chelation is the basis of the clinical 64Cu/67Cu-SARTATE and 64Cu/67Cu-SAR-bisPSMA agents and of numerous 64Cu-labelled antibody constructs (including sarcophagine-trastuzumab and sarcophagine-cetuximab conjugates for PET imaging), where head-to-head comparisons have shown less than 6% loss of 64Cu from sarcophagine-antibody conjugates in serum over 48 hours, matching or beating NOTA. A drawback of first-generation sarcophagines (SarAr) is that the residual positive charge on the copper-cage complex increases kidney retention of labelled antibody fragments; charge-modified analogues (AmBaSar, and dibromomaleimide-linked variants) reduce this off-target renal uptake while retaining the fast, room-temperature labelling that makes sarcophagines attractive for 64Cu (and increasingly 67Cu) theranostic pairs.",
