    tldr: "Attaching a small chemical 'hook' that grips the body's own albumin protein makes a radioactive drug stay in the bloodstream for longer, so more of it reaches the tumour before it is cleared out through the kidneys.",
    summary: "Small-molecule and peptide radioligands (such as PSMA-617) are cleared from the blood within minutes to a few hours, which limits the fraction of injected activity that can diffuse into and bind tumour tissue before excretion. Conjugating an albumin-binding moiety, most commonly a truncated Evans blue (EB) dye derivative or 4-(p-iodophenyl)butyric acid (IPBA/IP), to the targeting ligand allows reversible, non-covalent binding to circulating serum albumin (biological half-life around 19 days), which extends the ligand's effective blood half-life from minutes to several hours without permanently trapping the radionuclide.\n\nIn preclinical work, Evans-blue-modified PSMA-617 (EB-PSMA-617) showed markedly extended blood retention compared with unmodified PSMA-617 and produced higher tumour uptake and greater therapeutic efficacy at lower administered activity in mouse models; a single low dose eliminated PSMA-positive tumours in one widely cited study. Albumin-binder chemistry generalises across chelator and radionuclide (86Y/90Y/177Lu-DOTA-EB-PSMA-617; 64Cu-PSMA-CM using an MPA albumin-binding moiety) and across targets (SSTR, GRPR ligands). The main design trade-off is pharmacokinetic: too strong an albumin interaction raises blood and normal-organ (especially bone marrow) dose and can worsen the therapeutic index, so linker length, albumin-binding affinity and total charge are tuned empirically for each ligand; increased kidney or hepatobiliary retention has also been reported for some albumin-binder designs and must be balanced against the gain in tumour dose.",
